  "worked_solution": "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan\u2061x)={sec}^{2}\u2061x$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sin\u2061x)=cos\u2061x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan\u2061x+2sin\u2061x) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}\u2061x+2cos\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032}(0) & ={sec}^{2}\u2061(0)+2cos\u2061(0) \\\\  & =1+2(1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope is $3.$",
